Look through EXECUTE when choosing how to collect a result

An EXECUTE statement, SQL-level or generated for every execution of a prepared
statement, plans as a PhysicalExecute that holds the executed plan as a member,
not a child. Two checks of the plan root stopped at it:

  - GetSources() returned the PhysicalExecute itself, which supports no batch
    index, so an order-preserving result always used the sequential
    SOURCE_ORDERED collector: results were collected on one thread.
  - OrderPreservationRecursive() never saw an ORDER BY in the executed plan, so
    with preserve_insertion_order = false the unordered collector served it and
    ORDER BY results came back out of order.

PhysicalExecute now reports its plan's sources, and the order check looks
through EXECUTE. SQL-level EXECUTE returns ORDER BY results out of order in
every release tested from 1.0.0 to 2.0.0, and collects serially in 2.0.0. The
C and C++ APIs run every prepared execution as an EXECUTE, so both affect them
too.

